```java
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);

// Want: fetch user, then their orders, in parallel with their preferences,
//       combine and return.
Future<User> userF = pool.submit(() -> userService.fetch(id));
User user = userF.get(); // BLOCKS — worker waits idle

Future<List<Order>> ordersF = pool.submit(() -> orderService.fetch(user));
Future<Prefs> prefsF = pool.submit(() -> prefsService.fetch(user));

List<Order> orders = ordersF.get(); // BLOCKS
Prefs prefs = prefsF.get();         // BLOCKS

UserDashboard result = build(user, orders, prefs);
```

Problems:
- Three blocking `get()` calls — the caller thread sits idle.
- Sequential structure even though orders and prefs could overlap.
- Exception handling means try/catch around each get, with `ExecutionException` unwrapping.
- No way to say "do all of this asynchronously and let me know when done."

```java
Executor pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);

CompletableFuture<UserDashboard> dashboard =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> userService.fetch(id), pool)
        .thenCompose(user -> {
            CompletableFuture<List<Order>> ordersF =
                CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> orderService.fetch(user), pool);
            CompletableFuture<Prefs> prefsF =
                CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> prefsService.fetch(user), pool);
            return ordersF.thenCombine(prefsF,
                (orders, prefs) -> build(user, orders, prefs));
        })
        .exceptionally(ex -> defaultDashboard());

// Use it without blocking the caller
dashboard.thenAccept(this::renderResponse);
```

What's better:
- **No blocking** — the calling thread returns immediately.
- **Orders and prefs run in parallel** via `thenCombine`.
- **Exception path is declarative** — `exceptionally` handles failures from any stage.
- **Composition is fluent** — reads top-to-bottom like a description of the data flow.

Unlike `Future`, you can complete a `CompletableFuture` from any thread:

```java
CompletableFuture<String> cf = new CompletableFuture<>();

// Some other code path — callback from a third-party library, message arrived, etc.
listener.onMessage(msg -> cf.complete(msg.body()));
// or on failure
cf.completeExceptionally(new TimeoutException("no message"));
```

This enables wrapping callback-style APIs (Netty, OkHttp's async, JDBC drivers with callbacks) into a uniform `CompletableFuture`-based programming model. Most modern async libraries already return `CompletableFuture`s precisely for this reason.

**`completeOnTimeout(value, timeout, unit)`** (Java 9+) and **`orTimeout(timeout, unit)`** add timeout behavior without the awkward `CompletableFuture.allOf(actual, scheduledTimeout)` patterns from earlier.

